Zero the SAX handler before libxml2 is asked to initialise it

-[GSSAXHandler _initLibXML] allocates an xmlSAXHandler with malloc and hands
it straight to xmlSAX2InitDefaultSAXHandler, whose first act is

    if ((hdlr == NULL) || (hdlr->initialized != 0)) return;

so whenever the uninitialised `initialized` field happens to be non-zero, the
call does nothing at all and the handler keeps whatever the heap had in it.

In practice _initLibXML then calls xmlSAXVersion(), which re-establishes the
standard callbacks, so this is not usually visible as a parse failure -- the
cost is a read of uninitialised memory on every parse, an initialisation that
silently does nothing, and the remaining fields of the handler being whatever
was in that memory before.

Valgrind names it on every XIB load, since the xib unarchiver parses through
NSXMLParser and this handler:

    Conditional jump or move depends on uninitialised value(s)
       at xmlSAX2InitDefaultSAXHandler (libxml2)
       by -[GSSAXHandler _initLibXML]  (GSXML.m:3808)
     Uninitialised value was created by a heap allocation
       at malloc
       by -[GSSAXHandler _initLibXML]  (GSXML.m:3801)

calloc costs nothing here -- one structure per parser -- and makes the
initialisation deterministic.

A standalone reproduction is in gnustep-patch-repros/sax-handler-init.m: six
frames under valgrind, and silent with this patch applied.

--- a/Source/Additions/GSXML.m
+++ b/Source/Additions/GSXML.m
@@ -3798,7 +3798,10 @@
  */
 - (BOOL) _initLibXML
 {
-  lib = (xmlSAXHandler*)malloc(sizeof(xmlSAXHandler));
+  /* Zeroed, not merely allocated: xmlSAX2InitDefaultSAXHandler() returns
+   * without doing anything when the handler's `initialized' field is not
+   * zero, so an uninitialised one is silently left uninitialised. */
+  lib = (xmlSAXHandler*)calloc(1, sizeof(xmlSAXHandler));
   if (lib == NULL)
     {
       return NO;
